WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.049
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.049 --> 00:00:09.280
This is one of those new, absolutely insane
papers from the Google DeepMind guys.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.259
You are going to see a followup work to an
algorithm that looks at a bunch of images

00:00:14.259 --> 00:00:20.400
and from that, it automatically learns the
concept of birds, human faces or coral reefs,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.819
so much so that we're able to write a new
sentence, and it will generate a new, close

00:00:25.819 --> 00:00:29.029
to photorealistic image from this written
description.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:33.079
This network is capable of creating images
that are significantly different than the

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:35.129
ones it has been trained on.

00:00:35.129 --> 00:00:37.899
This already sounds like science fiction.

00:00:37.899 --> 00:00:39.070
Completely unreal.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:42.039
This work goes by the name PixelCNN.

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:44.760
We'll discuss a followup work to that in a
moment.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.780
The downside of this method is that these
images are generated pixel by pixel, and many

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.690
of these pixels depend on their neighborhoods.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:57.640
For instance, if I start to draw one pixel
of the beak of a bird, the neighboring pixels

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.089
have to adhere to this constraint and have
to be the continuation of the beak.

00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:05.030
Clearly, these images have a lot of structure.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:09.799
This means that we cannot do this process
in parallel, but create these new images one

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:11.140
pixel at a time.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:16.320
This is an extremely slow and computationally
expensive process, and hence, the original

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.750
paper showed results with 32x32 and 64x64
images at most.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.790
As we process everything sequentially, the
execution time of the algorithm scales linearly

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:29.890
with the number of pixels we can generate.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:34.820
It is like a factory where there are a ton
of assembly lines, but only one person to

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:37.430
run around and operate all of them.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:42.729
Here, the goal was to start generating different
regions of these images independently, but

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:47.009
only in cases when these pixels are not strongly
correlated.

00:01:47.009 --> 00:01:50.030
For instance, doing this with neighbors is
a no-go.

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:55.120
This is possible, but extremely challenging,
and the paper contains details on how to select

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.090
these pixels and when we can pretend them
to be independent.

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:02.840
And now, feast your eyes upon these spectacular
results.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:08.149
If we're looking for "A yellow bird with a
black head, orange eyes and an orange bill",

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:11.190
we're going to see much more detailed images.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.840
The complexity of the new algorithm scales
with the number of pixels not linearly, but

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.720
in a logarithmic manner, which is basically
the equivalent of winning the jackpot in terms

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.760
of parallelization, and it often results in
a more than 100 times speedup.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.150
This is a factory that's not run by one guy,
but one that works properly.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:35.730
The lead author, Scott Reed has also published
some more amazing results on twitter as well.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:40.030
In these examples, we can see the evolution
of the final image that is generated by the

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:41.030
network.

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.980
It is an amazing feeling to be a part of the
future.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.300
And note that there is a ton of challenges
with the idea, this is one of those typical

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:53.090
cases when the idea is only the first step,
and execution is king.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:55.920
Make sure to have a look at the paper for
more details.

